Current bug report: {'summary': 'PAX header entry name ending with / causes problems', 'description': 'There seems to be a problem when a PAX header entry (link flag is \'x\') has a name ending with "/". The c0.m0() check ends up returning true because of the trailing slash which means no content can be read from the entry. PAX header parsing effectively finds nothing and the stream is not advanced; this leaves the stream in a bad state as the next entry\'s header is actually read from the header cont'}

Recent bug reports (SBERT retrieval results with similarity): [{'similarity': 0.38, 'summary': 'Handle NULL terminated GNU AR extended name', 'description': 'We have an AR archive (a .lib file) whose extended name is terminated by a NULL instead of a line feed which causes an IOException ("Failed to read entry: 0"). It looks like ArArchiveInputStream.getExtendedName just needs to check namebuffer[i] for \'\\012\' *or* 0. The ar tool in latest GNU binutils seems to be able to handle this.'}, {'similarity': 0.13, 'summary': 'PureJavaCrc32C constructor calls overrideable method', 'description': "Constructors must not call overrideable methods. An object is not guaranteed fully constructed until the constructor exits, so the subclass override may not see the proper parent object. In this case, the class is package-protected so it's possible to ensure that subclasses don't override the method."}, {'similarity': 0.39, 'summary': 'Defective .zip-archive produces problematic error message', 'description': 'A truncated .zip-File produces an java.io.EOFException conatining a hughe amount of byte[]-data in the error-message - leading to beeps and crippeling workload in an potential console-logger.'}]

